                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Short version: 30%[/h1]\nViking Saga 3 has a few good ideas left in it, but the level design feels too tired, repetitive, and vexing to enjoy any of them.\n\n[h1]Long version:[/h1]\nRepetition is a large part of time/resource managers, but good games can make it feel like a fun challenge instead of pure tediousness. Viking Saga III: Epic Adventure is not such a good game.\n\nThe core is the same as in the previous Viking Sagas: we build houses for rental income, which we then spend on building production buildings so we can gradually clear the map of all obstacles.\nFor the most part, the balance in resources is great; with some planning, we will never really lack any of them. Additionally, their flow is constant enough to keep the stages going without any downtime.\n\nThe problem is that what we do is not fun. The longer we play, the more tiresome gimmicks appear, which force us to repeat the same sub-tasks constantly. All of this is there to mask the weakening level design—it is as if the developers completely ran out of ideas, so they reused and significantly toned up some cheap tricks to hide the game’s shortcomings with even worse decisions.\n\nI wish there were any redeeming features here, but beyond the solid gameplay basics, the finale of the Viking Saga does nothing but disappoints.",
